<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>DW Environment</title><subtitle type="html">News, Analysis and Service from Germany and Europe - in 30 Languages</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss_en_environment</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:42:54Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss_en_environment" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-11798?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss_en_environment" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>The hidden environmental cost of AI data centers</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3b0e6f1f8329bd289010dd35134c8e9535a9a341292b389e1fc4ec70049a5436</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:42:54Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T11:08:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/the-hidden-environmental-cost-of-ai-data-centers/a-78789889--12be241c4f53.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/the-hidden-environmental-cost-of-ai-data-centers/a-78789889?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">As data centers multiply to meet AI demand, they're straining power grids, draining water supplies and reshaping the land and air around them.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>Can communities be proteced from melting glaciers?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:02ba6287d20f7b96cbd9663793bd0b89782b2bfef3c2397ce869ab679c61f152</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-09-01T13:18:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/as-glaciers-melt-can-communities-be-protected/a-78615775--188b1bb14c63.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/can-communities-be-proteced-from-melting-glaciers/a-78615775?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Rising temperatures are increasing the risk of floods and avalanches in mountain regions. The disaster in Nepal and Tibet shows the urgent need for solutions, and the tools that could help save lives.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>Inside the sea cucumber smuggling boom</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8e31cc5ffebb79a738c2b52d4d19ab1f20751a0c9bce1ea05db01936b615165b</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-31T10:50:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/inside-the-sea-cucumber-smuggling-boom/a-78005662--31767cc80c27.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/inside-the-sea-cucumber-smuggling-boom/a-78005662?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">A quiet multimillion‑dollar black market is exploding across the Indian Ocean, driven by one unlikely animal: the sea cucumber.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>What 'The Odyssey' can teach us about climate chaos</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9dacc73bfc9dd99d32997beb093a256c7a4ce67f20c21dcabd9627665d3cded5</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-28T12:52:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/what-the-odyssey-can-teach-us-about-climate-chaos/a-78545407--a4283ef6c536.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/what-the-odyssey-can-teach-us-about-climate-chaos/a-78545407?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The Bronze Age collapse may have started with a water crisis. The droughts of today raise the unsettling question of whether it could happen again.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>The great lake divide: Collapse vs. comeback</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:90b9baf8ebf636a2a21ded5d10415764cb877af9992ee2af4a8dc691f3c9a01d</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-27T09:38:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/the-great-lake-divide-collapse-vs-comeback/a-78460263--98d8fd43b28f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/the-great-lake-divide-collapse-vs-comeback/a-78460263?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">As some of the world's biggest lakes vanish in real time, others are proving that recovery is still within reach.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>Was the heat of this summer really 'normal?'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:79994b74754ccae03db964286203fef930ed879e07a6d0505412c9604f4508c7</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-26T16:44:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/was-the-heat-of-this-summer-really-normal/a-78513682--f848e035fb1c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/was-the-heat-of-this-summer-really-normal/a-78513682?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Some political messaging denies the role of human-caused climate change in this summer's extreme heat. The science tells a different story.</summary><category term="Climate" /></entry><entry><title>Trees are vital for cooler cities, but they need help too</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f76b6af3bacc4544a1d88c0bc0841abe178ab091289c259333fe5138cee68568</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-25T09:39:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/trees-are-vital-for-cooler-cities-but-they-need-help-too/a-78488665--3c6c418b5255.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/trees-are-vital-for-cooler-cities-but-they-need-help-too/a-78488665?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Record-breaking heat and drought are putting urban trees at risk. Experts explain how to protect these essential assets and build city forests that can withstand the climate challenges ahead.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>How Europe can better protect against extreme wildfires</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fdd034c9821cd765ea2c6bcc99ca5c61809d7062b09ee8dfd20697f51aba201f</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-20T09:00:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/how-europe-can-better-protect-against-extreme-wildfires/a-78435243--328a5026ab21.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/how-europe-can-better-protect-against-extreme-wildfires/a-78435243?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">In another summer of European wildfires that have even seen wetlands burning, scientists say the situation is set to worsen in the future. What's needed is fire prevention and climate protection.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>In drought-stricken France, tankers supply drinking water</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4626d546ddad419fb679a51a2ac8ee8c3fecebbb4006a7f8b1175d7a71a69aa8</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-19T04:33:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/in-drought-stricken-france-tankers-supply-drinking-water/a-78420016--f1ca7862089b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/in-drought-stricken-france-tankers-supply-drinking-water/a-78420016?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">France is seeing water shortages in regions that usually have plenty of supply. This summer's extreme heat is exposing how unprepared much of the country, and Europe, is for increasing water scarcity.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>How to grow food for more people in a hotter world</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:35f935e1b4b84caa752ea22e2ff8daed4a95ea3355b5ed6919fdc0769a2f8faf</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-18T13:22:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/how-to-grow-food-for-more-people-in-a-hotter-world/a-78413324--d5bd37cc31a0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/how-to-grow-food-for-more-people-in-a-hotter-world/a-78413324?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Inside futuristic labs, plants are scanned, stressed and analyzed. The aim is to use the results to produce plant strains that can better serve a growing population in a warming world.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>Can the cool north handle a surge in climate tourism?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4860127d9415e1c6fabeb50308d8864c76d73ebec4efb436e35b5a67f130384d</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-17T11:03:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/can-the-cool-north-handle-a-surge-in-climate-tourism/a-78362462--ea94ebd3a88f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/can-the-cool-north-handle-a-surge-in-climate-tourism/a-78362462?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">With heat records continually being broken and southern summers getting hotter, travelers are heading north. Fragile ecosystems are paying the price.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>Can we go green without forever chemicals?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:16ee1fb3090f73e9010f0f2934dc22ff34debb93aa576d9c21e4ec1e4d806d80</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-13T07:13:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/can-we-go-green-without-forever-chemicals/a-77888704--d9d50592f16d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/can-we-go-green-without-forever-chemicals/a-77888704?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">As nations globally move to ban forever chemicals, should renewable energy technologies be exempt for the sake of saving the climate?</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>How one German city plans to challenge climate extremes</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:aecefc557699604cca78ae1dc64ae890e4172b6f0d1e6aab890becd7c54a91a5</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-11T07:25:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/how-one-german-city-plans-to-challenge-climate-extremes/a-78278373--0e8e71e797bb.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/how-one-german-city-plans-to-challenge-climate-extremes/a-78278373?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Western Europe has recorded its hottest-ever June and July, and extreme weather from drought to storms is here to stay. How can we adapt to a fundamentally altered climate? Cities like Kiel are trying new ideas.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>Eco wins: Hungary goes solar, marmots go viral and Rome gets an upgrade</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7466678d5b0dc3a9f62a4dd4d1353f618a963c7d52f00b0e283fb6499b1b4cd4</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-07T14:06:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/eco-wins-hungary-goes-solar-marmots-go-viral-and-rome-gets-an-upgrade/a-78281783--1a471e4e39e5.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/eco-wins-hungary-goes-solar-marmots-go-viral-and-rome-gets-an-upgrade/a-78281783?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">From Hungary's accidental solar revolution to marmot crowdfunding and a long-overdue upgrade for Rome's big snake, good news comes in strange packages this week.</summary><category term="Climate" /></entry><entry><title>Drought threatens power supplies in Danube countries</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d9028c22eb3faa3c88e285fd1e19b6e637bf20f24a3374e9c735bf6e199a1105</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-06T19:55:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/drought-threatens-power-supplies-in-danube-countries/a-78269916--26f97f256e9e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/drought-threatens-power-supplies-in-danube-countries/a-78269916?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Droughts, heat and falling water levels in the Danube are hitting central and southeastern Europe hard. Hungary and Romania are experiencing an energy crisis — whereas countries like Bulgaria are better prepared.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>The hidden problem with small modular nuclear reactors</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:84e44795c94a8bacd1f4ce8e7af58df83e1c77f2b4f94d757b260682947d4512</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-05T12:54:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/the-hidden-problem-with-small-modular-nuclear-reactors/a-77491854--9c1aceb7337f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/the-hidden-problem-with-small-modular-nuclear-reactors/a-77491854?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Mini nuclear reactors are seen as a flexible energy source. Their promise: abundant energy, cheap, clean and climate-friendly with lower water use. But do the numbers add up? We look at what's behind the SMR hype.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>South Sudan: How climate change can result in toxic water</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:489695167fcefca6d472a68f4b737e4aae8673e65f6b973f1e89260090e0a355</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-04T14:46:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/south-sudan-how-climate-change-can-result-in-toxic-water/a-78193418--de689e4bad86.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/south-sudan-how-climate-change-can-result-in-toxic-water/a-78193418?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Years of flooding have resulted in disastrous water pollution in South Sudan's main oil region. As communities, scientists and health workers scramble for solutions, the long-term impacts of the crisis is yet to emerge.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Germany: As temperatures rise, so does climate skepticism</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e2961b6ad8d02fcea009b11a1b05d026fb27db3d1d7d615248c0629d27ff6bd5</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-08-02T08:44:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-as-temperatures-rise-so-does-climate-skepticism/a-78206354--820ea670e767.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-as-temperatures-rise-so-does-climate-skepticism/a-78206354?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Some 98% of active climate scientists say climate change is happening, and is man-made. Nonetheless, a new study shows the number of climate-change skeptics in Germany has increased. Why is that?</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>What's really causing Europe's wildfires</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1534ddc415e975fe5dc7b2749b30c40a38995a7b40ab0629eefffbfedb12c3f7</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-07-31T16:10:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/what-s-really-causing-europe-s-wildfires/a-78194776--90fad987906d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/what-s-really-causing-europe-s-wildfires/a-78194776?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">From Greece to France, Europe is burning. But what's the real reason for the historic blazes ripping through the continent and why are they getting harder to stop?</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>Eco wins: From death strip to nature haven and other green surprises</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ab98ef30d4bf72b40797e89d0c84fb5397fce692a2f0fb02cc3cdb8fa186470e</id><updated>2026-09-05T04:41:03Z</updated><published>2026-07-31T12:33:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/eco-wins-from-death-strip-to-nature-haven-and-other-green-surprises/a-78187601--c4ed72c7de9e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/eco-wins-from-death-strip-to-nature-haven-and-other-green-surprises/a-78187601?maca=en-rss_en_environment-22607-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">A Cold War relic becomes a biodiversity haven, a Pokemon Go for trees and seagrass gets a second life. This week's eco wins come from some unexpected places.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry></feed>
